dropped the body
--
libc++ 18's basic_string(basic_string &&) initializes `__r_' through an
immediately-invoked lambda `[](basic_string &__s) -> decltype(__s.__r_) &&
{ ...; return std::move(__s.__r_); }(__str)' (<string> line 925).  The
lambda's body was dropped ("invalid implicit conversion from 'struct
__compressed_pair &' to 'struct __compressed_pair &&'"), so a moved-from
std::string was nondet under libc++ -- the moved string's size and elements
read as garbage.  Preceded by "symbol '__compressed_pair_elem' does not
uniquely resolve" in __compressed_pair's implicit copy constructor (two
private bases that are specializations of one template); the header-free
shape of that ambiguity alone (compressed_pair_elem<T1,0> and <T2,1> bases,
a lambda returning `decltype(s.r_) &&') converts correctly, so the full
libc++ context is needed to reproduce.  Under libstdc++ the same program
passes.  Audit residue item 1 (round 162), 11 libc++ tests.
Round 163b (cvise reduction to 14 lines, /tmp/r163/red): three defects.
(1) The lambda's body, type-checked in place, was not marked converted, so
the end-of-unit sweep completing half-converted system-header bodies
converted it a second time and the re-check failed
(cpp11_lambda_body_in_system_header_converted_once).  (2) `std::string
("abc")[1]' was nondet even without a move: libc++ sizes its
`__constexpr_memmove' with `__libcpp_datasizeof<char>::value', which is
clang's `__datasizeof(char)' -- unknown to the front end
(cpp11_datasizeof_builtin) -- or, without the builtin, an offsetof over a
member class template instantiated from the static member's initializer
while the enclosing instance is still being converted, which produced an
empty struct (cpp11_member_class_template_in_static_member_initializer).
All three fixed; the test is CORE.
